## 4. Rollback plan

**Blast radius.** The probe writes exactly one file: `reviews/spikes/sakg-s1-extraction-probe.md`.
No writes to `constitution/` (Z0 is read-only; sole legal writer `kernel/amendment.py`,
`constitution/README.md:3-4`). No writes to `ledger/` or `reviews/tasks/` — the corpus is read-only
input; mutating a sealed goal to make it more extractable would corrupt the very evidence the
probe measures. No process management, no state directories touched (see §7).

| id | trigger | action | recovery point |
|---|---|---|---|
| RB-1 | `ProbeConfig` mutated after `frozen_at` | Declare `S-INVALID` (design §6.2). Discard **all** `FieldVerdict[]` — they were scored against a moved threshold. Restart from W1 with a new `frozen_at` and a note recording the invalidated run. | W1 |
| RB-2 | <3 sealed goals, or all share one `goal_kind`, or §4.3 fails to parse | Declare `S-INVALID` (corpus/schema cannot support the spike). Do **not** substitute synthetic goals or hand-author a field list. Emit a NO-GO report citing R-3 where the schema is at fault; otherwise report the corpus shortfall and stop. | W0.1 / W0.2 |
| RB-3 | W6 audit fails (missing citation, unresolvable path, missing row) | Return only the offending rows to W5.* (design §6.1 self-loop). Rows that passed stay scored. If a row cannot be substantiated on retry, it reverts to the seed value `absent` — never to `extractable`. | W5.* (partial) |
| RB-4 | Arithmetic or rule-application error found in W7/W8 | Recompute from the published `FieldVerdict[]`. W7 and W8 are pure functions of W6's output, so no extraction is redone. | W7 |
| RB-5 | Deliverable written but accept checks fail | If the failure is formatting → fix formatting, rewrite the file, re-run. If the failure is `loss_ratio: 1.0` vs. the regex → **do not alter the number**; leave the file as written, mark the run `BLOCKED-ON-G-1`, escalate via W10. | W9 |

**Global abort.** At any point before W9, aborting leaves the repo unchanged — every unit
W1–W8 produces in-session records, not files. After W9, rollback is deletion of the single
deliverable file. There is no partial-write hazard: the report is written once, complete, at W9.

**Irreversibility check.** Nothing in this plan is hard to reverse. The one genuinely
irreversible act available to the executor is *publishing a GO that was manufactured* — by
skipping fields (blocked by I-2/E-4), by scoring inferences as `extractable` (blocked by §5.1
rubric + I-1/E-6), or by moving the threshold after seeing the data (blocked by W1-before-W2 +
RB-1). Those three are the rollback plan's real subject.

